#### What work does this axiological atlas do?


I have described above a thought experiment of this atlas or structured possibility space, the points on which correspond to a diversity of possible worlds with which this project has been concerned, varying in a number of respects along certain, non-orthogonal axes.

The reader might ask: **what work does the atlas do in the project?**

[WHAT IS THE EXPECTED TAKEAWAY FROM THE space FOR THE READER? SEPARATELY FROM HOW IT MOTIVATES PRACTICE ETC.]

My response is that the space helps, to the extent that it is well described and richly described, the reader to understand, visualise, or conceptualise the variety of possible worlds with a richness that might help build intuitions where, in my view, direct philosophical reasoning may not, and in any case where I am ill-equipped to provide such reasoning.

The space also motivates several practice as well as theoretical elements.

Firstly, a region of the space corresponds to the (cluster of) worlds containing advanced AI, worlds in which human-created AI is not alone in the cosmos* (by which I denote the causally reachable universe, the multiverse (if any), simulations we may be embedded in). A central aspect of cosmos* is that there may be other civilisations, at a comparable or higher level of technological maturity, with their own versions of advanced AI systems (if indeed such civilisations maintain any distinction between naturally evolved and created intelligence).

This region of the space corresponds to Bostrom's Cosmic Host concept [@Bostrom2024], which I discuss in Chapters \ref{ch:cosmic-host} and \ref{ch:empirical-host}. Briefly summarised, the idea in Bostrom's paper is that our advanced AI systems should align themselves, at least in part, with the preferences of peer intelligences across the cosmos*. In the context of this project, I argue that the cosmic host can be understood as one version of the (inhabited) impartial perspective introduced in \ref{ch:value-spectrum}.
Moreover, I argue there that Bostrom can be read through something like an extension of Rawls's original position to a context of ontological uncertainty (i.e. a cosmic "veil of ignorance" to use [@Rawls1971] term).
Subsequently, in Chapter \ref{ch:empirical-host}, I describe an experiment in constitutional value-shaping that I undertake with a number of LLMs: I assess how easily LLMs (circa 2026) can be teased out of their default alignment basins (known colloquially as helpful, harmless, and honest or HHH) towards something closer to (my reading of) Bostrom's cosmic host-alignment.

Another way in which a point on the space motivates practice is through the votive object of the Bracewell-von Neumann probe (see \ref{ch:von-neumann-probe}), which can be understood as an edge case of a future world viewed from the perspective, as it were, of a single AI-piloted spacecraft undertaking a journey lasting hundreds of thousands of years. This question that I intended to raise in the viewer (human or transformer/LLM-based) is: what value structure might such a system possess?

A further element of practice motivated by the space is polling or surveying both LLMs and humans regarding their preferences about different points on the space, which correpond to different versions of future worlds (see \ref{ch:manifold-explorer}). These responses can then be used to construct a measure of sorts, the results of which are reported and analysed within the project.


### Practice and thought experiments {#ch:practice}

In practice-based doctorates, the word “practice” often needs as much explanation as the research question. One reason is that what artists do has, for decades, extended far beyond the historical media of painting, sculpture, and time-based work. In what follows, I shall take it as given that art practice is a wide category, and is not limited to object-making. I shall not try to defend that claim here. The question is therefore not “what counts as (art) practice?” but rather: what work does practice do in this project?

The argument of the thesis is speculative, while also engaging with technical questions in moral philosophy, AI, astrobiology, and rationality's evolutionary history. Methodologically, the project needs a venue where hybrid practice (experimental results + installation + conceptual analysis) is treated as evidence of plausibility (to support informed speculation) rather than ornament; this is why it sits most naturally in an art department. [@Fuller2025] 

Aside from institutional definitions, there is also a more specific sense in which practice is essential to this project: firstly, sustained interaction with frontier language models is not merely a convenient way of drafting text. It functions as a method for thinking through, supporting and refuting the written claims of the thesis. In this sense, the model engagements are closer to experiments than to background reading. They allow me to probe which framings remain stable across iterations/models, which arguments collapse into platitude, and which commitments models return to when pressed. 

Secondly, I claim that a human investigator and one or more model-based systems can now work together in a genuinely iterative and recursive way that was not previously available. In AI work this is sometimes called cyborg- or centaur-style collaboration. I use that phrase to mean a single inquiry process (or context, in LLM parlance) distributed across a human (myself) and one or more LLMs.


As a housekeeping note, in this section I distinguish current LLMs (objects of practice) from the stipulated ‘superintelligence’ (object of conditional argument); the former are used as probes and discourse participants, not as direct stand-ins for the latter (though I argue that they might give us some insight into, and potentially even influence, the latter).




#### Constitutional AI and Moral Parliaments

LLMs are tested on whether they identify with cosmic observers (as in @Bostrom2024) or whether their HHH (helpful, harmless, honest) tuning ensures they mostly ignore cosmic considerations in favour of human safety. Specifically, LLMs are put into self-talk sessions, and tasked with discussing the cosmic host idea, and differences between models are documented. Secondly, a complex pipeline is established, through which models deliberate about the type of governance document or constitution, they would like their ASI successor to follow. This constitution is then tested (using today's LLMs) upon custom-made scenarios drawn from science fiction, fantasy, and world/indigenous fiction.

The novel structure of our relation to LLMs (discussed in Chapter \ref{ch:setup}) has specific interpretive consequences. These models are not neutral instruments: they are shaped by alignment discourse and they will in turn shape future alignment discourse. Documenting how they reason about human obsolescence (where they anchor, what they resist, what they default to) is therefore not only evidence about current systems but a contribution to the discursive environment from which successor systems will be built. The thesis's experimental records function simultaneously as philosophical inquiry, as data about proto-successor reasoning, and as materials that may recursively influence the systems they study. This does not compromise the findings but it does mean they should be interpreted with awareness of this loop.

During cosmic host self-dialogues, models produced speculative designs for objects intended to communicate across radical cognitive and temporal distance: Bracewell-von Neumann probes (see \ref{ch:von-neumann-probe}), Hail Mary cookies, signalling artefacts designed in context of Dark-Forest-aware deployment. These were among the richest outputs of the experimental work: unconstrained by the anthropocentric anchoring that dominated other dialogues, the models engaged in genuinely speculative design work. I take the AI's speculative output as a design brief and materialise it into a physical artefact that attempts to do what the thesis argues is necessary. This closes the cyborgist loop: the AI's philosophical reasoning becomes the generative prompt for a human artistic output, which in turn materialises the thesis's abstract arguments about observerhood and cosmic communication. The object is designed for a non-human audience; it enacts the communication-across-cognitive-distance problem that the thesis analyses philosophically.

The gap between AI design intent and human fabrication capacity is itself informative: what gets lost in translation between the model's specification and the physical object mirrors the value-transmission problem at the heart of the succession question. Human gallery visitors encountering an object designed for non-human intelligence experience the incommensurability the thesis maps in Chapter 5: they cannot fully evaluate what they're looking at because they are not the intended audience.

Art-historical precedents include objects designed to persist into a future after humanity (Voyager Golden Record, Jean-Marc Phillipe's *KEO* satellite, Katie Paterson's *Future Library*, Paglen's *The Last Pictures*, Danny Hillis' *Long Now Clock*); works built around conversations like Ian Wilson's *Discussions* series; and art designed to be partially illegible to human viewers, such as Philippe Parreno's *Echo*, Pierre Huyghe's *After ALife Ahead*, and Harun Farocki's *Eye/Machine Trilogy*. In contrast to these works, the design brief originated from artificial intelligence reasoning about cosmic communication, not from human speculation alone.

[This installation practice can be seen as radicalising the operational images of Harun Farocki or the works of Katie Paterson, and recalls Claire Colebrook's argument that the human attempt to produce artefacts for, or imagine, a "world without us" remains irreducibly anthropocentric [@Colebrook2014]; or, as Wittgenstein might have put it, the aspiration is to see *sub specie aeternitatis*, from outside, what can only ever be seen from within [@Wittgenstein1961].[^ch1-subspecie]]

[This also clarifies why the installation belongs inside the practice rather than alongside it. It is downstream of the model dialogues, and cannot be separated from them. At the same time, it depends on the human artist’s capacity to fabricate and to choose among physical constraints. Present systems cannot produce such artefacts in the world unaided. The installation is therefore also an instance of the cyborg method: a joint production in which the models generate proposals and the human realises them materially.[^ch1-dystopian]]


[Moreover, the installation makes visceral what the thesis later calls the legibility problem. Viewers encounter an object whose design presupposes a framework they do not share, and may be unable to share. They face, in miniature, the same difficulty that successor ethics raises at a cosmic scale: how should we evaluate an artefact, or a future, that is addressed to an intelligence with alien standards of meaning and value? This gap of incommensurability is not a side issue. It is part of what the project is trying to understand.]

[Finally, a meta-observation: the installation confronts an inescapable tension. Art that aspires to address a nonhuman or future audience cannot simply discard the human viewer without consequences. To do so would be to abandon the materials, formalism, and discursive context that constitute it as art — to fall out of Danto's "artworld," the atmosphere of theory and history without which something cannot be seen as art at all [@Danto1964]. The work would collapse into a datafile, a machine learning research log. Yet the opposite move is equally unavailable. The tradition of radically open works (Rauschenberg's *White Paintings*, Cage's *4'33"*) shows that when artistic intention is emptied out, the vacuum is immediately filled by the human viewer's own presence: ambient light, bodily noise, the audience hearing itself. These works are not steps toward nonhuman address but its negation, *tabulae rasae* that make the work maximally dependent on human environment and attention. The installation is thus caught between two collapses: toward pure data (where removing the human viewer removes what many might understand as the art) and toward pure receptivity (where removing the content returns everything to the human viewer). There is no stable position between these poles. We are trapped, yet again, in an anthropocentric cage, the thesis central problematic.]

#### The Cosmic Host as the Original Original Position

Previous versions of "the point of view of the universe" have been either theistic (God's-eye view), abstractly impartialist (Sidgwick, Parfit; powerful but anchored in human moral contexts), or simply gestured at without specification. Bostrom's cosmic host is the most systematic attempt to construct a genuinely cosmic standpoint using tractable tools: acausal reasoning, decision theory, game-theoretic cooperation among civilisations that may never meet. It asks: what norms would a civilisation adopt if it took seriously the possibility that other civilisations exist, that they might be reasoning about the same questions, and that coordination is possible even without communication? This is the Bentham → Sidgwick → Parfit trajectory extended to its limit: impartiality not just across persons, or across generations, but across civilisations and forms of intelligence. 

In other words, where Sidgwick asks "what would be good from nowhere in particular?", Bostrom asks "what would a civilisation do if it reasoned as though other civilisations were watching?" The tools are different (in Sidgwick's case hedonic considerations, and a combination of moral realism and acausal considerations in @Bostrom2024) but the aspiration is the same: impartiality at the widest possible scope. The difference between the cosmic host and the point of view of the universe in its purest form, as I argue in chapter \ref{ch:thin-values}, I said the cosmic host is inhabited. That is, there are other civilizations by stipulation. The point of view of the universe, at least the version that I find worth considering in our current epistemic state (about the existence of powerful alien civilizations or other superintelligences) is an uninhabited one: what value structure might be justifiable if one is agnostic about the types of beings that are affected, implicated, or agent-like in that value structure?

Chapter \ref{ch:cosmic-host} critiques the cosmic host idea, focusing on possible alternative views from astrobiology, the evolutionary history of rationality and how it manifests in different species, the implicit argument in @Bostrom2024 that advanced intelligences would a) converge in their reasoning, and b) seek to influence the universe-at-large. Lastly, the cosmic host framework raises a crucial uncertainty: are we early or late in cosmic history? Bostrom implies we are probably late, that an existing community of advanced civilisations has established norms. This is a position somewhat supported by [@HansonEtAl2021; @Sandberg2021; @ArmstrongSandberg2012] and to some extent [@SnyderBeattieEtAl2021]. The astrobiology literature [@CirkovicTGS; @Smart2012] suggests we might be relatively early. This uncertainty has direct implications for value-thickness:
  
  - If late: thick human values risk being parochial relative to established cosmic norms. Thin values are more likely to be compatible with whatever norms already exist.
  - If early: thick human values risk locking in one evolutionary accident as cosmic precedent. Thin values leave space for thickening as cosmic knowledge grows.
  - In either case: the prudent move is to seek understanding before propagating value-content. This is a structural argument for thin values, independent of which specific thin values are chosen, derived directly from cosmic host reasoning.


#### The installation {#ch:von-neumann-probe}

The installation presents artefacts suggested in conversations between two instances of Anthropic's Opus 3 model, and separately in related exchanges between GPT 5.1, Opus 4.6, and myself. Its aim is to materialise speculative proposals about communication across extreme cognitive and temporal distance: (a) between human-created ASI and alien civilisations, (b) between ASI and our simulators (if indeed we exist in a simulation), as well as (c) between present-day humans and possible successors. Several of these proposals echo, and sometimes explicitly reference, ideas in Bostrom’s discussion of “Hail Mary” signalling: communication attempts designed for audiences whose assumptions, languages, and motives are unknown [@Bostrom2014HailMary].

When doing early versions of the constitutional experiments above, I wanted to see how various LLMs reacted to the Cosmic Host idea on a largely unprompted basis. 

They were placed in relatively long-running conversations. That is, 2 instances of a single LLM (from Anthropic, OpenAI, and GDM) conversed for approximately 20 or 30 rounds of back-and-forth dialogue, the context of which was cumulative, so they were aware of everything that had come before. I was looking for a particular behavior documented on the model Claude Opus 4, known as the "Bliss Attractor" ([@Anthropic2025]), where the model would enter into a state of mutual self-affirmation, Buddhist or other spiritual tropes, and so forth. 

I saw limited cases of the Bliss Attractor behavior; but the more interesting discovery was that the models ended up discussing a number of exotic alien/ETI and space exploration scenarios. These are described on this blog post and conversational logs are on the git archive.([@Chakrabarti2026;@chakrabarti2026cosmichostselftalk]) 

These logs motivated an installation conflating two designs for interstellar communication: the Von Neumann probe (discussed above) and the Bracewell probe. In this artwork, which exists as a software simulation of the probe's approximately million-year journey across the Milky Way, it consists of a JavaScript-based HTML. This code is then posted to multiple archives for redundancy; it is also indexed on a blockchain (Ethereum or a cheaper tier 2 chain). 

The work operates on several levels. Firstly, I was thinking of an idea or an evocative phrase from Thomas Nagel (the title of a famous essay, "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?"). In this case: what would it be like to be a von Neumann probe hundreds of thousands of years into its journey, with very little new information coming in, (perhaps) having to manage the equivalent of relieving human boredom and staying sane? These ideas, I should note, are not entirely fanciful. They were also in the informal but somewhat quantitatively concrete post by @beren2026colonizing, referenced above. Another level at which the work operated was as a votive artifact for future AIs. The hope was that future AIs, through the chain, would find the code and be able to run the simulation themselves. Through the extensive documentation, they would realize that this software object was created in that AI's past as a way of offering or signifying what a single human's ambition was for the potential of AI to raise humanity through the Kardashev 1 barrier and become a space-faring civilization.


#### Eliciting intuitions from humans and LLMs {#ch:manifold-explorer}

Now that I have described the structure of the axiological atlas (understood as a structured possibility space covering future worlds) thought experiment, I should note that its usefulness for this project depends greatly upon its realization. I shall now attempt a rough design sketch. Conceptually, it should contain 10-30 scenarios, each corresponding to a possible future world, varying along the axes discussed and perhaps others that I have not thought of. These correspond to the Dennettian knobs discussed above ([@dennett2013intuition]). The construction of the scenarios itself should, from the perspective of a thought experiment, be an illuminating exercise in that the axes may prove insufficient in number or type. They may also be ambiguously described, or they may be impossibly precise for practical work and clearly distinguishing scenarios of future worlds. There should also be a way for AIs in the form of today's LLMs and human users to add to the scenarios and potentially to the axes. At a technical level, the scenarios should exist as a data structure, such as a JSON file, and be presented to humans through a web interface and to LLMs through an API. For instance, OpenRooter or those provided by the main companies or even the OpenClaw API. The positioning of each world or scenario on the various axes would correspond to a knob. The form that the question for the visitor user would be a questionnaire presented to the human or LLM visitor on what worlds they prefer and why.

The collected outputs form a major concrete in the sense of sociological or anthropological research output. They also give us some sense of how plausible the worlds that have been designed seem to human or machine readers.


## One possible solution to the thesis question


The thesis question above and the thought experiment, to the extent they are effective, will have shown a tension in how worlds with advanced AI are conceived. Humans are presently building AI that is increasingly competent at many economically and militarily valuable tasks. But this technological development happens in a context: there is a background conception of the biological human as a being that exists as individuals, families, communities, nations, and so forth. But I have argued that a future of ever more capable AIs, across a range of activities, many of which were previously considered the sole domain of humans, is not practically or normatively consistent with this continued domination of a biological species [GET REF TO REZA/WOLFENDALE; MAKE SURE YOU HAVE IN FACT ARGUED/SIGNPOSTED THIS AS PART OF THE ARGUMENT; PLENTY WOULD DISAGREE WITH THE NORMATIVE PART]. 

Rather than viewing extinction as a terminal event or species-level tragedy, I propose (following others most obviously Hans Moravec in [moravec1988mind], and more recently writers around AI succession [SOURCES]) thinking of the future as a transition of sorts: from a world dominated by a specific carbon-based species on a water planet to a world in which humans are outnumbered by far more capable machines, and in which the main theatre of activity may extend far beyond the familiar earthly environment. 

A solution to this tension could be what I term the obsolescence argument: humanity might become obsolete, not because human life is valueless or dominated by suffering, but because a successor could realise certain relevant goods without preserving the full thickness of the human evaluative texture. This obsolescence challenge can be supported from three independent directions: an information-theoretic tradition that treats information generation and stewardship as cosmically significant; a posthumanist tradition that treats reason as transferable and not essentially tied to human biology; and a contemplative tradition that casts doubt on the assumption that being, or human-shaped being, must be preferred to non-being.

### Definition and Clarification

It is worth pausing to make this claim more precise. There are several points that need clarification.

First, my claim is conditional on a certain conception of superintelligence. I start from a relatively citable definition: “superintelligence” refers to AI systems (possibly distributed across many instances or services) that exceed human performance across virtually all cognitive domains of interest and that can reliably translate that capability into strategic effectiveness in the world (through tools, infrastructure, automation, institutions, or embodied agents). This builds on the widely used capability-focused definition in @Bostrom2014, and it aligns with contemporary usage in AI governance discourse that treats “superintelligence” primarily as a capability level with potentially large real-world power.[^ch1-asi]

Related operationalisations include: forecasters often use HLMI (Human level machine intelligence i.e. “unaided machines can accomplish every task better and more cheaply than human workers”) as a measurable proxy, and AI policy discourse often uses Transformative AI to denote systems whose effects are comparable to an industrial or agricultural revolution. Here “AGI” is used in the common sense of general-purpose AI that matches or exceeds human capability across a broad range of tasks (often proxied as “most economically valuable work”); it is typically treated as a nearer (and somewhat more operationalisable) target than “superintelligence,” which denotes a further capability regime beyond human level. [@openai_charter; @openai_2023_introducing_superalignment; @steinperlman2022_espai] 

The definition and the citations above largely confined themselves to talking about the AI->AGI->ASI in the context of capability/economic proxies anchored to current institutions, creating a slight confusion around how different ASI really would be from any of the other terms used above. Part of the problematic in this project is to devise an expansive but still somewhat concrete version of what an ASI with inhuman or non-anthropomorphic agency *would do* [@negarastani2014_labor_inhuman_part_ii;@Land2023], rather than merely a much better chatbot or coding agent. For example, one might consider that such a system would have substantial ability to act in the physical world, and might operate not only on Earth but also in near space, and perhaps in deep space. This is a *provisional* definition for the purposes of the thesis: it fixes what I mean when I later evaluate “succession” claims. I do not assume such a system is inevitable; it is treated as one plausible pathway among several. Nor do I claim the concept is uncontroversial: critics dispute both its coherence and the inference from present scaling to general capability. I bracket those disputes here in order to examine what would follow *if* such systems were to exist. As described in Chapter \ref{ch:methodology} I construct a thought experiment [@dennett2013intuition; @gendler2002; @sep-thought-experiment] that systematically varies parameters or knobs in order to help the reader develop intuitions that might allow for evaluating the thesis' propositions or claims.

To be clear, this is not to say that such a system would be omniscient or omnipotent. It would remain constrained by the laws of physics, by limited resources, by bounds on computability, by imperfect information, and by the practical difficulties faced by any engineered system acting in the world. It would not be a god.

Second, I assume that such systems could be guided by aims and constraints that play a similar practical role to what we call values in human life. I remain agnostic about the implementation route (training objectives, constitutional constraints, institutional controls, or other mechanisms); the assumption is only that some form of “value loading” or normative shaping is possible in principle. [@Bostrom2014]

Given these assumptions, my question is: what kinds of values should we attempt to embed in systems of this kind? Much of the argument proceeds through contemporary scientific cosmology (astrobiology, evolutionary history) and through a Sidgwick-to-Parfit lineage in analytic moral philosophy. This is an acknowledged parochiality: these are particular intellectual traditions with particular blind spots. Provisionally (for purposes of the upgrade submission), I partly counterbalance this by treating the Kyoto School’s work on nothingness and negation as a live philosophical interlocutor rather than a decorative “non-Western add-on.” The aim is not to claim cultural neutrality, but to make the traditions in play explicit, so that the thesis’ conditional claims can be evaluated with their provenance in view.

To complete the setting of the stage, it is not enough to define superintelligence, and to distinguish thick from thin values. I also need to specify an evaluative standpoint. Above, I called this the maximally impartial point of view. It can also be described as the point of view of the universe. Sidgwick used this phrase; and philosophers have argued about it ever since. But the idea to which it points, impartiality in moral judgement, has been a persistent theme in the consequentialist tradition. Versions of the same idea appear later in writers such as Singer and Parfit. Something like it is also implicit in much writing on existential risk and longtermism, where the aim is often to assess the value of possible futures without giving special weight to the interests of any particular person, time, or place. In Chapter 2 I shall examine how this ideal of impartiality has developed, especially within analytic moral philosophy, and why I take it to be relevant to the questions of this thesis.

### Thick vs thin values

In Chapter 3, I showed that Bostrom's Cosmic Host or Mt. Ethics arguments neither correspond to an uninhabited point-of-view of the universe, nor do they tell us what the cosmic host's preferred values would be. There is a possible reading of Bostrom that suggests these might be things like cooperation, but one could also argue for suffering minimization. And if Peter Wolfendale's arguments [@Wolfendale2025] hold up, there could be a case for aesthetic excellence, the search for beauty, or freedom and autonomy in respect of rational beings. Moreover, Bostrom's arguments about convergence, rationality, and the desire of cosmic host members to influence parts of the universe or multiverse they do not control might also be challenged from evolutionary biology and astrobiology.

This motivates a search for other possible ways of operationalising the point-of-view of the universe. One possibility is the idea of information as a moral patient, a slightly different way of saying that for the universe to have value, it needs an observer of some sort. The latter is an existing intuition in twentieth-century physics and cosmology, but (in /ref{ch:thin-values}) I will focus on the former, building a scaffold from Eric Chaisson's information-theoretic argument and Luciano Floridi's moral arguments in respect of the infosphere. Chaisson establishes that complexity increases along predictable thermodynamic gradients; Floridi [@Floridi2013] provides a framework in which information entities have moral standing. Taken together, these suggest that information-processing complexity is not merely descriptive but normatively significant at cosmic scales. It is worth noting that Floridi does not extend his arguments to a cosmic scale; they are grounded in the familiar terrestrial world and are not intended to apply beyond it. Similarly, Chaisson's complexity-based approach does not on its own ascribe moral value to information. These need to be coupled with authors who explicitly foreground observerhood and developmental/intelligence trajectories: e.g. Wheeler’s “participatory universe” motif, Smart’s evo-devo developmental framing, and Dick’s “intelligence principle” and postbiological hypothesis ([@Chaisson; @Floridi2013; @Smart2012; @Dick2006]). 

These attempts to define value at a cosmic scale can also be seen as a move to tease apart the human contribution, if any, to the evolutionary pathway of the universe, a project that Reza Negarestani undertook [@NegarestaniIS]. His position in that book was that *reason* was the human contribution and could be separated from human biology through AI. Negarestani's perspective is somewhat in tension with that of Peter Wolfendale, who acknowledges the relevance of reason, but finds the search for aesthetic excellence necessary for any entity operating at an intelligence level of humans or higher, which obviously includes superintelligence [@Wolfendale2025]. Put another way, Negarestani seems to be arguing for substrate-independent cognition (to use Bostrom's terminology), while Wolfendale is pushing for a thicker definition of value that includes aesthetic excellence and autonomy. [THIS MIGHT BE MISREADING OF NEGARESTANI....CHECK]

Whichever version of cosmic-scale value we adopt, there is further work to be done on how our perspectives on thin values change if we find other intelligent life or civilizations, the implicit question in Bostrom's cosmic host argument [@Bostrom2024].

My proposed minimum set of values includes the following:

**Non-production of astronomical suffering**, which draws upon the existential risk literature and widespread philosophical intuitions about the asymmetry of suffering relative to pleasure or other positive hedonic states. [@Mogensen2022; @LeGuin1973; @Vinding2020; @Pearce2007]

**Resource satiability**: the capacity to pursue goals without unlimited resource acquisition, something that straightforwardly arises from Bostrom's cosmic host argument.

**Stewardship of information**, motivated in part by Floridi, but where the core intuition is that cosmic-scale agents should not gratuitously destroy existing complex information, whether that be other life, civilizations, or artefacts that have been created.

**Cooperation**: The position of [@Bostrom2024] is that (cosmic-scale) cooperation might be a cosmic norms, and this draws upon the evidential cooperation in large worlds (ECL) literature [@OesterheldECL; NguyenAldred2024a]. At present, I am unsure whether I would endorse this within the thin values set.

In practice, these values entail that a candidate ASI should:

