[^ch1-asitimeline]: Public forecasting tools and journalism give a rough sense of how compressed "superintelligence timelines" have become in contemporary discourse. For example, a Polymarket question on whether OpenAI will announce it has achieved AGI before 2027 was trading around the low-teens percentage range (i.e., a minority probability as of late 2025). On Manifold, a market on whether "unambiguous artificial superintelligence" will exist by 1 Jan 2030 was priced at roughly ~30% (with other ASI-by-2030 markets lower), reflecting both nontrivial expectation and substantial uncertainty. The forecasting site Metaculus, meanwhile, showed an approximately 2-year time gap between a weak AGI and superintelligence as defined in the relevant entry (see link for definitions). Meanwhile, mainstream coverage often frames timelines through named forecasters: e.g., The Guardian reported an updated forecast that set 2034 as a revised horizon for "superintelligence" (without committing to a "human destruction" date), illustrating both the salience of nearish timelines and their volatility. [@down_2026_guardian_ai_timeline_delays; @manifold_yudkowsky_asi_by_2030_2027; @polymarket_agi_before_2027_2025; @metaculus2020_weakagi_to_superintelligent_oracle]

[^ch1-asi]: It's debated whether superintelligence (ASI) and the related artificial general intelligence (AGI) are terms with coherent definitions. For instance, Melanie Mitchell explicitly frames AGI and "superintelligence" as speculative and notes that these views differ from those studying biological intelligence, i.e., the concept is contested and not settled. DeepMind’s “Levels of AGI” paper is basically motivated by definitional ambiguity: it surveys existing AGI definitions and proposes an ontology/taxonomy precisely because “AGI” is used inconsistently and is hard to benchmark. [@morris2023_levels_of_agi; @mitchell2024_debates_agi; @brennan_kak_west2025_artificial_power]

[^ch1-bostrom]: Bostrom is a central interlocutor here, but his role is contested. A racist email he wrote in the 1990s resurfaced publicly in 2023; he issued an apology and repudiation. The episode, and wider controversies around longtermism and the institutional legacy of the Future of Humanity Institute, have shaped how this body of work is received. In engaging Bostrom’s ideas, I neither ignore this context nor treat it as decisive refutation: the thesis evaluates arguments while remaining attentive to the intellectual and institutional conditions under which they became influential. [@bostrom2023_apology_old_email; @guardian2024_fhi_closure]


[^ch1-dystopian]: Some might diagnose a more dystopian reading of the practice. One of the speculations of what human labour might look like after cognitive tasks have been largely automated is as fabricators and manipulators of physical objects that AI systems struggle with (the hand, with its fine network of nerves and muscles, has proven particularly resistant even to recent progress in robotics). So in this scenario, humans are essentially teleoperated by cloud-based AI systems. [@aria2024_robotic_dexterity_thesis; @liu2024_dexterous_hands_matter; @wired2023_add_humans_teleoperation]

[^ch1-intergalactic-spreading]: One of the more concrete specifications for how human-originated missions could rapidly, at least in cosmological terms, spread beyond the Milky Way is developed by Stuart Armstrong and Andrew Sandberg in [@ArmstrongSandberg2012]. Their exploratory-engineering study argues that a mature, highly automated civilisation could initiate settlement not merely of the Local Group but of much of the causally reachable universe. Their design is different from other von Neumann probe implementations because it does not require the starships to stop at intermediate points to replicate. Instead, their propose an enormous initial fan-out: probes are launched directly toward each reachable galaxy and, after arrival, construct local replicators and dispatch a second wave throughout that galaxy, allowing them to theoretically reach all stars that humanity can causally reach (owing to the expanding universe, most of the universe cannot be reached by ordinary, physically-plausible sub-light methods). In this document, the authors were trying to show that rapid colonization of nearby galaxies is theoretically possible within billions of years, using technologies that humanity could foreseeably and plausibly develop over thousands or millions of years, and that the total mass and energy and mass required (to construct and launch the probes respectively) is available in the Solar System. It does not, however, discuss in detail what the implications for the AI systems running the probe might be; a subsequent, more informal treatment by Beren Milledge take more conservative assumptions (higher weight as a result of shielding against relativistic dust particles and therefore higher energy requirements, if speed is held constant, and longer launch period) and briefly discusses the high level of intelligence needed to autonomously guide and complete the mission. However, the referenced post was more concerned with how a civilization might move from Kardashev 2 (K2) to K3, which is largely beyond the frame of interest in this project; I ask what happens as we go from our current state (approximately Kardashev 0.75) to K2.[@beren2026colonizing] 

[^ch1-moore]: For instance, if the Mona Lisa were somehow transplanted or teleported to a corner of our universe that was not causally reachable by any humans or any other entity that could perceive it or that have the capacity to experience it, what would it mean for it to have value? One perspective could be that value requires subjects who can form evaluative judgments and therefore, and so the question as stipulated is meaningless. Another perspective could be that its rare and intricate and intentional configuration of atoms confers a value by virtue of its structured information content. Yet another could be that the fact that it has been created through a process distinct from the natural physical evolution of the universe, and this is what gives it value. Both of the latter examples presuppose an evaluative standpoint that is outside of experiencing subjects, which is a somewhat foreign concept in the Western analytic tradition. Obviously, this question of intrinsic aesthetic value cannot be settled in a footnote. Let's see this source for a survey of more in the context of aesthetic good.